@meta
id: 29-06
title: Changing one pair into another
chapter: 29
type: lesson
skills: surround
par: 20
minutes: 5
vscode: find and replace on quotes, and pray about the apostrophes
open: messages.ts
vimrc: set packpath^=. | packloadall!

@brief
Same plugin as last lesson, shipped in the same place —
`pack/dojo/start/surround/plugin/surround.vim`, sitting in this
directory. Still nothing installed.

Its second verb is *change surround*: take the pair that is already
around this text and make it a different pair. Not delete-and-retype.
One command, two characters: the one you have and the one you want.

The style guide moved to double quotes. `messages.ts` has five strings in
single quotes. Convert them. The text inside must not change — that is
the point of having a verb for it rather than a search and replace, which
would also have eaten the apostrophe in any message that had one.

@keys
| `cs{old}{new}` | change the surrounding `{old}` pair into a `{new}` pair |
| `cs'"` | single quotes become double quotes |
| `cs"(` | double quotes become brackets |
| `qq` … `q` then `4@q` | five of anything, recorded once |

@hints
`cs` needs two characters after it: what is there now, and what you want
instead.
--
`cs'"` on the first string. Then do not do it four more times by hand —
chapter 21 gave you a way to record it.
--
`j` to the first message, then `qq` `cs'"` `j0` `q` records one line's
worth. `4@q` finishes the other four.

@solution
    j  qq cs'" j0 q  4@q  :wq<Enter>

Seventeen keystrokes for five strings.

`cs` is the verb that has no equivalent anywhere else, and it is the one
that changes how you write. The alternatives are all worse:
`:%s/'/"/g` would have destroyed any apostrophe in the prose; selecting
the string and retyping it costs the whole string; `f'x` at each end costs
four moves and leaves you hunting for the second quote.

Note what it does *not* need from you: the cursor was at the start of the
line each time, not on a quote. `cs'` uses vim's own `a'` text object,
which finds the quoted string on the line for you. The plugin is not
clever here — it is delegating to something vim already had, which is why
it is eighty lines instead of eight hundred.

The pairs it knows are the ones you would guess: `(`, `[`, `{`, `<` and
their closers, plus any other single character used on both sides. In the
real vim-surround the open bracket adds padding spaces and the closer does
not — `cs"(` gives `( x )` and `cs")` gives `(x)`. The version shipped
here never pads. Worth knowing before it surprises you on a real machine.

@start pack/dojo/start/surround/plugin/surround.vim
" dojo-surround.vim - a small, honest surround plugin.
" Shipped inside this lesson. You did not install it; the dojo did.
"
"   ds{char}           delete the surrounding pair
"   cs{old}{new}       change one surrounding pair into another
"   ys{motion}{char}   surround the text the motion covers
"   {Visual}S{char}    surround the selection
"
" Brackets come in pairs: ( [ { < all give you the matching closer.
" Anything else is used on both sides, so " gives "..." and ` gives `...`.

if exists('g:loaded_dojo_surround')
  finish
endif
let g:loaded_dojo_surround = 1

let s:pairs = {
      \ '(': '()', ')': '()',
      \ '[': '[]', ']': '[]',
      \ '{': '{}', '}': '{}',
      \ '<': '<>', '>': '<>',
      \ }

function! s:delims(char) abort
  return get(s:pairs, a:char, a:char . a:char)
endfunction

" The text between the pair, without the pair itself.
function! s:inner(char) abort
  let save = @z
  let @z = ''
  silent execute 'normal! "zyi' . a:char
  let text = @z
  let @z = save
  return text
endfunction

" A visual selection covering the pair AND what is between it. vi" selects
" the inside; o h o l then walks each end out by one character onto the
" delimiters. Doing it this way rather than with va" matters: va" also
" swallows the whitespace next to the string.
function! s:select(char) abort
  return 'vi' . a:char . 'ohol'
endfunction

function! s:replace(char, text) abort
  let save = @z
  let @z = a:text
  silent execute 'normal! ' . s:select(a:char) . '"zp'
  let @z = save
endfunction

function! s:ds(char) abort
  let inner = s:inner(a:char)
  if inner ==# ''
    return
  endif
  call s:replace(a:char, inner)
endfunction

function! s:cs(old, new) abort
  let inner = s:inner(a:old)
  if inner ==# ''
    return
  endif
  let d = s:delims(a:new)
  call s:replace(a:old, d[0] . inner . d[1])
endfunction

function! s:ys(type) abort
  let d = s:delims(nr2char(getchar()))
  let save = @z
  if a:type ==# 'line'
    silent execute "normal! '[V']\"zy"
    call setreg('z', d[0] . "\n" . @z . d[1], 'V')
    silent execute "normal! '[V']\"zp"
  else
    silent execute "normal! `[v`]\"zy"
    let @z = d[0] . @z . d[1]
    silent execute "normal! `[v`]\"zp"
  endif
  let @z = save
endfunction

function! s:vs(char) abort
  let d = s:delims(a:char)
  let save = @z
  silent execute 'normal! gv"zy'
  let @z = d[0] . @z . d[1]
  silent execute 'normal! gv"zp'
  let @z = save
endfunction

nnoremap <silent> ds :<C-u>call <SID>ds(nr2char(getchar()))<CR>
nnoremap <silent> cs :<C-u>call <SID>cs(nr2char(getchar()), nr2char(getchar()))<CR>
nnoremap <silent> ys :<C-u>set operatorfunc=<SID>ys<CR>g@
xnoremap <silent> S :<C-u>call <SID>vs(nr2char(getchar()))<CR>

@start messages.ts
export const messages = {
  orderNotFound: 'That order does not exist.',
  orderAlreadyShipped: 'That order has already shipped.',
  refundWindowExpired: 'The refund window has closed.',
  paymentDeclined: 'The card was declined by the issuer.',
  webhookInvalid: 'The signature did not match the payload.',
};

@expected messages.ts
export const messages = {
  orderNotFound: "That order does not exist.",
  orderAlreadyShipped: "That order has already shipped.",
  refundWindowExpired: "The refund window has closed.",
  paymentDeclined: "The card was declined by the issuer.",
  webhookInvalid: "The signature did not match the payload.",
};
